Miller its Jensen the way I play Deus Ex  mankind divided might not be the same  way you play when I play Adam Jensen I  avoid conflict drink all the alcohol I  find and stop bad guys from doing  terrible things by releasing pheromones  into the air and then asking politely  but every obstacle in the cyberpunk  world of mankind divided has multiple  solutions provided you unlock certain  abilities and the game rewards you for  achieving things with their own  personally preferred method no matter  what that may involve for conflict there  are more than enough tools for a direct  approach a variety of lethal and  non-lethal guns grenades and ammo which  fire and explode wonderfully and helpful  cybernetic augmentations that can give  you temporary buffs like heavy armor or  slowing down time alternatively there  are robust stealth mechanics filler you  slip quickly from cover to cover see  enemy sight lines or straight up turn  invisible for a while  you could also use hacking skills and  have fun taking advantage of the game's  futuristic setting turning century  robots  automated turrets and the environment  against aggressors but no matter how you  decide to go about solving a problem in  Deus Ex  there's always a thrill when you succeed  and the game dishes out experience  points no matter which actions you take  this means killing enemies is as  satisfying as slipping past them without  raising suspicion punching through a  wall is just as fulfilling as stealing a  keycard and using the door the great  thing is there's no superior way to  complete a task mankind divided also  introduces a handful of new augmentation  abilities for Jensen on top of those  introduced in the previous game human  Revolution some of these new orbs are  more interesting than others though  remote hacking for example is an  invaluable tool for disabling automated  obstacles at a distance and the Icarus  Dash is a great tool for discrete  traversal  however the Tesla peps and nano blade  abilities achieve similar effects to  some of the game's more conventional  weapons they stun and kill enemies from  a distance there are some advantages to  using them but most of the time I wish  I'd spent points elsewhere mankind  divided continues with the series's  portrayal of a cyberpunk dystopia with a  focus on the politics around people with  cybernetic implants and the subsequent  implications on the definitions of  humanity the plot involves countless  government bodies corporations security  forces underground resistances the  Illuminati and the events of day sex  frame suspicion on literally every  organization and individual in  this makes it impossible to trust any  one character other than Jensen as he  attempts to find evidence revealing the  motives and masterminds behind a number  of terrorist acts deciding which  organisation to aid and which to deceive  during pivotal plot moments is grueling  because everything is so unyieldingly  gray but the lack of a clear antagonist  or driving purpose can lead to temporary  disillusionment with no one enemies  standing in the crosses for too long the  upside to this is that mankind divided  doesn't feature the ill-received  boss battles that appeared in human  Revolution and remains purely as a  series of increasingly challenging  infiltration and investigation scenarios  all of which can be solved in the manner  of your choosing the world of days x is  an easy one to get completely absorbed  in the city hubs all based in Prague are  where you'll spend most of your time and  are stunningly well realised the streets  are full of life and bowled me on  advertising the futuristic buildings are  constructed among old world European  architecture and dilapidated housing and  make the city believable  you'll revisit locations and walk the  same routes regularly gaining an  intimate knowledge of the streets and  structures and this knowledge is  incredibly useful and satisfying to draw  upon later in the game as new situations  arise one hub is a stunningly  claustrophobic and oppressive  multi-level slum filled with ramshackle  apartments and dense marketplaces which  maybe just want to stop every few feet  just to soak it all in spaces are filled  with character densely packed with  discoveries to make about its  inhabitants and are exceptionally  curated with subtlety you might say push  some unremarkable boxes in a shop aside  to pleasantly discover that someone has  hidden some rainy day cash behind them  you might also notice that despite is  constant over-the-top broody demeanor  Adam Jensen absolutely loves eating  colorful sugary cereal additionally a  portion of the game's side quests are  not explicitly marked and are only found  by going a day away to talk to certain  people you may have otherwise run right  past mankind divided also include an  asymmetrical online early competitive  mode called breach breach uses the  first-person combat stealth and remote  hacking mechanics of Deus Ex in brief  visually abstracted challenge missions  with focus on both score chasing  speedrunning and beating other players  the tone of breach is very different to  the main camp  with the emphasis not so much on the  breadth of options available to you but  taking the path of least resistance  after playing through the main story  campaign as a pacifist breech felt like  it gave me much more license to  experiment without consequence racing  around like a maniac shooting non-human  enemies in the face and causing general  chaos as I tried to climb the speedrun  leaderboard as he progressed new  conspiracy stories arise and unlocking  the details requires you to successfully  collect gems in particular missions  these are interesting to take in but  feel out of place within breach but turn  all confusion aside breach is still a  pleasing way to hone your skills with  deus exes action mechanics Deus Ex  mankind divided refines and reinforces  the defining foundations of the series  it creates challenging situations and  gives players the tools and flexibility  to deal with them in a multitude of ways  all within an absorbing cyberpunk world  although it's not a significant  departure from human Revolution mankind  divided is still a uniquely fulfilling  experience and one which feels rare in  games today
 

